Good — I understand the test framework. Let me run the existing test suites to make sure baseline is green, in the background, while I design.

Now let me also check whether smAgent.js (Jira SM) needs this too — the ticket subject says "Tick arming-side action (gh-807, js/common/reviewVerdicts.js consumers) + the merge gate". Given the incident fleet and the merge gate being machineSmAgent/mergeBot, and to keep change minimal, I'll implement:

1. `reviewVerdicts.js`: pure classification + selection (`classifyThreadSeverity`, `selectResolvableThreads`, `buildThreadAckReply`). Hmm — should these live in reviewVerdicts? It's "verdict record mapping" — yes, L1 tests live in test_reviewVerdicts.js. 

2. `machineSmAgent.js`: decideActions new action + executeAction wiring + machineAuthor config.

3. `smProvider.js`: github `reviewThreadList`, `replyToThread`, `resolveThread`.

Also mergeBot? mergeBot only merges CLEAN; after threads resolve, GitHub recomputes mergeable_state → CLEAN; next event/tick merges. The SM tick merge happens in machineSmAgent. OK — mergeBot doesn't need changes (it'll merge once CLEAN). Actually wait: mergeBot runs on events; thread resolution creates timeline activity? Resolving threads doesn't fire pull_request events, but the SM tick (cron) handles it. Fine.

Should I also handle the case in decideActions where mergeState is BLOCKED for reasons OTHER than conversation gate (e.g. required review from a human CODEOWNER)? Resolving machine suggestion threads is idempotent and bounded; the ack+resolve only touches eligible threads; if still BLOCKED afterwards, next tick skips again. No harm.

Where exactly in decideActions: in the `has('pr_approved')` branch, replace the final else with: push resolveSuggestionThreads + keep skip? Return both actions: `[{type:'resolveSuggestionThreads'}, {type:'skip', reason:...}]`? executeAction handles each; the skip just logs. Simpler: when mergeState is BLOCKED → resolve action INSTEAD of skip; other non-mergeable states (UNKNOWN etc.) → skip. Hmm, but UNKNOWN/BEHIND handled separately. Let me write:

```js
if (has('pr_approved')) {
    if (pr.mergeState === 'BEHIND') {
        actions.push({ type: 'updateBranch', ...});
    } else if (pr.mergeState === 'CLEAN' || pr.mergeable === true) {
        actions.push({ type: 'merge', ...});
    } else if (pr.mergeState === 'BLOCKED') {
        // gh-828: unresolved-thread conversation gate — resolve machine-owned
        // non-blocking threads in-tick...
        actions.push({ type: 'resolveSuggestionThreads', ...});
    } else {
        actions.push({ type: 'skip', ...});
    }
    return actions;
}
```

AC1 sim: "approved head with 4 suggestion threads ⇒ after one tick all 4 resolved + one ack comment; mergeState leaves BLOCKED." — Hmm, "mergeState leaves BLOCKED" still puzzles me. Maybe it means the merge state is re-checked and leaves the BLOCKED branch (i.e., next tick proceeds to merge)? Or the sim has other blockers (checks pending) so it remains BLOCKED but threads resolved? Either way, my implementation: BLOCKED → resolve action. If still BLOCKED next tick, it retries (idempotent; eligible count 0). Good.

Also should resolve happen even when mergeState CLEAN? If a repo has no conversation-gate requirement, threads don't block the merge; resolving them anyway is harmless but noisy. Keep to BLOCKED only — minimal, per ticket ("the conversation gate held every head BLOCKED").

Edge: BLOCKED may be due to pending required checks (fresh head). Resolving suggestion threads then is still fine (they're approved-with-suggestions machine threads; the merge gate still waits for checks). But is it right to resolve threads while the head might still change? Threads on an approved head — pr_approved is sticky; the verdict record is for the current head. If head moves, pr_approved stays (sticky) and the verdict record lookup for the new head fails → gate closed. In decideActions BLOCKED branch, state.pr.headSha is the current head; resolve uses records for that exact head. Safe.

Wait, one more subtlety: machineSmAgent's approved branch fires only when checkConclusion is green/none (red/pending return earlier). The incident PRs were ai_validated (green). OK.

Also the `resolveSuggestionThreads` action when there are no verdict records (pre-gh-807 PRs) → resolvable list empty → no-op, but we should log why. executeAction logs.

